   "i": 108,
   "shape_h": "blockquote",
   "shape_m": "blockquote",
   "html_text": "Determining regions of rejection The diligent researcher considers all 6-tuples of success and failure as the sample space, and takes their numbers as sufficient statistic. The event of six successes, or six correct guesses, has a probability of ⟦M⟧ under the null hypothesis that the student is merely guessing, against a probability of ⟦M⟧ under the alternative hypothesis. If we set ⟦M⟧ , then only this sample of six successes is included in the region of rejection of the null hypothesis. Sample",
   "md_text": "Determining regions of rejection The diligent researcher considers all 6-tuples of success and failure as the sample space, and takes their numbers as sufficient statistic. The event of six successes, or six correct guesses, has a probability of ⟦M⟧ under the null hypothesis that the student is merely guessing, against a probability of ⟦M⟧ under the alternative hypothesis. If we set ⟦M⟧ , then only this sample of six successes is included in the region of rejection of the null hypothesis. Sample"
  },
  {
   "i": 6,
   "shape_h": "blockquote",
   "shape_m": "blockquote",
   "html_text": "The tea tasting student Consider a student who claims that they can, by taste, determine the order in which milk and tea were poured into the cup. Now imagine that we prepare five cups of tea, tossing a fair coin to determine the order of milk and tea in each cup. We ask the student to pronounce the order, and we find that she is correct in all cases! Now if she is guessing the order blindly then, owing to the random way we prepare the cups, she will answer correctly 50% of the time. This is our",
   "md_text": "The tea tasting student Consider a student who claims that they can, by taste, determine the order in which milk and tea were poured into the cup. Now imagine that we prepare five cups of tea, tossing a fair coin to determine the order of milk and tea in each cup. We ask the student to pronounce the order, and we find that she is correct in all cases! Now if she is guessing the order blindly then, owing to the random way we prepare the cups, she will answer correctly 50% of the time. This is our"
  },
  {
